Gozar on OpenWrt
================

The package installs three things: the headless Go core at /usr/bin/gozar-core,
a procd service at /etc/init.d/gozar, and its settings in /etc/config/gozar.
The LuCI pages live in the separate luci-app-gozar package.

Requirements
------------

  * The kmod-tun package, because the tunnel needs a TUN interface.
  * A Mozilla account that is allowed to use the proxy.
  * About 12 MB of free flash for the core; the binary is statically linked, so
    no extra libraries are needed.

Configuration
-------------

Edit /etc/config/gozar:

  config gozar 'main'
      option enabled '1'
      option email 'name@example.com'
      option password 'the password'
      option code ''          # only for accounts with two-factor login
      option server ''        # empty means the fastest location

Then run:

  /etc/init.d/gozar enable
  /etc/init.d/gozar start

The service signs in the first time it starts, writes the session to
/etc/gozar/Gozar/config.json, and then keeps the tunnel up. Every start after
that reuses the stored session.

The same settings are available in LuCI under Services -> Gozar, with the
state and the last log lines on the details page.

Notes
-----

The tunnel owns the default route while it runs. Stop the service
(/etc/init.d/gozar stop) to get the plain connection back; the service restores
the routes and the DNS settings it changed.

This package carries the core only. The graphical application is a desktop
Flutter build and is not part of the OpenWrt package.

CI limitation
-------------

The OpenWrt package is installed and started in the official rootfs container,
and the smoke test checks that /etc/init.d/gozar starts the gozar-core process.
However, a live-tunnel test is NOT performed in CI. Users must test the package
in a real environment: connect, verify the exit IP, and verify teardown/route
restoration on their own router.
